1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O c5 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. e3 Bf5 9. b3 Qc8 10. Re1 (10. Bb2 Ne4 11. Rc1 Nxc3 12. Bxc3 Be4 13. d5 $14 {Botvinnik-Najdorf, ol 1954}) 10... Rb8 11. Bb2 Rd8 12. Qe2 $6 (12. d5 Na5 13. Nd2 {followed by 14 e4 leaves White better placed than in analogous position arising from 8 d5.} (13. e4 $2 Nxe4)) 12... Bg4 13. Qd2 a6 14. d5 Bxf3 15. Bxf3 Ne5 16. Bg2 Qg4 $1 17. Qd1 (17. h3 Nf3+) 17... Qxd1 {Black has a slight advantage in the ensuing ending due to his mobile Q-side pawns and the possibility of entering a "good" knight v. "bad" bishop ending after the exchange of dark squared bishops.} 18. Raxd1 b5 19. f4 Ned7 20. cxb5 axb5 21. Bf3 Ne8 22. Re2 c4 23. b4 Nc7 24. e4 Nb6 25. e5 Kf8 26. Kg2 Ra8 27. a3 Na4 28. Nxa4 Rxa4 29. Re3 Ne8 30. h4 Rc8 31. Ra1 Ra7 32. Bd4 Rac7 33. Bc3 Ra7 34. Bd4 Rac7 35. Rc3 f6 $1 {Forcing the ending mentioned above.} 36. exf6 Bxf6 37. Bxf6 Nxf6 38. a4 $2 bxa4 39. Rxa4 Rb8 40. g4 $2 Rb5 41. Ra5 Rxb4 42. Kf2 Rb3 43. Rc2 c3 44. Ke3 Rb2 45. Kd3 Rxc2 46. Kxc2 Rc4 47. g5 Rxf4 $2 {Simply retreating the knight would have left the white K-side pawns at Black's mercy. The "pure" rook ending is more difficult to win.} 48. gxf6 Rxf3 49. fxe7+ Kxe7 50. Ra7+ Kf6 51. Rxh7 Rh3 52. Rh8 Ke5 53. Rg8 Kxd5 54. Rxg6 Kc5 55. Rg8 Kd4 56. Rg4+ Kc5 57. h5 d5 58. Rg8 d4 59. Rc8+ Kd5 60. Rd8+ Ke5 61. Re8+ Kf5 $2 (61... Kf6 $19 {Black wants to play ...Rh2+; Kc1 (Kd3? Rd2+ winning immediately), after which his king can gain access to d3. 61...Kf6 would allow this, e.g.} 62. Rd8 (62. Rf8+ Ke7) 62... Rh2+ 63. Kc1 Ke5) 62. Rf8+ Kg5 63. Rd8 Rh2+ 64. Kc1 Rd2 65. Rd5+ Kh6 {Source: CHESS, 28 May 1955, Vol.20/247, p355} 1/2-1/2

